    "title": "DEC announces swimming advisory at Turtle Island on Lake George - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Natalie St. Denis ,\n1 hours ago\nRelated video: 2 people bitten by beaver on Turtle Island\nLAKE GEORGE, N.Y. ( NEWS10 ) — The New York State Department of Environmental Conservation (DEC) has announced a water safety advisory for Turtle Island on Lake George. The DEC is warning swimmers to avoid areas around campsites 1-5 at Turtle Island through at least July 14.\nDownload our WTEN+ streaming app on Roku, Fire TV and Apple TV! The DEC says the advisory is due to the presence of one or more beaver that may be infected with rabies. It comes after two people were bitten by a beaver just offshore from a dock between campsites two and three on Turtle Island on July 4.\nBoaters and campers should exercise caution and are advised to avoid contact with beaver while recreating. DEC continues to closely monitor the colony and requests visitors report any sightings of beaver in the area demonstrating unusual behavior.\nGet the latest news, weather, sports and entertainment delivered right to your inbox! Individuals are reminded to never approach or feed any wildlife. Unusual animal behavior should be reported by calling 518-623-1200. In an emergency, call 911 or NYSDEC Dispatch at 518-897-1326. For more information about rabies, visit Rabies: What to Know .\nRead the latest from NEWS10:\nNEWS10 is the Capital Region’s local news leader!\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to NEWS10 ABC.",
    "summary": "By Natalie St. Denis , Related video: 2 people bitten by beaver on Turtle Island LAKE GEORGE, N.Y. ( NEWS10 ) — The New York State Department of Environmental Conservation (DEC) has announced a water safety advisory for Turtle Island on Lake George. The DEC is warning swimmers to avoid areas around campsites 1-5 at Turtle Island through at least July 14. It comes after two people were bitten by a beaver just offshore from a dock between campsites two and three on Turtle Island on July 4.",
